  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Monday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: High pressure continues to spread west \nas the week progresses. Thunderstorm potential still exists today \nalong the Utah border and in northeastern Elko county but chances \nare low, at about 5 to 10 percent. Chances persist for tomorrow \nbut will be even lower than today. The high pressure system is \nexpected to expand far enough west by Thursday to keep moisture \nout of the forecast area. Smoke from both local and Pacific \nNorthwest wildfires is also expected to have local impacts during \nthis time, particularly in northern Nevada. A very smoky morning \nwas observed in Elko today and several more are expected to \nfollow. Daytime southerly to southwest flow is expected to help \nclear smoke during the days, however calm or northerly flow during\nthe nights will allow smoke to increase for the late night to \nearly morning hours. Smoke concentrations are expected to be high \nenough to cause issues for sensitive groups, especially during \nearly morning when concentrations will be highest. The final focus\nof this forecast will be the heat. Temperatures are expected to \nclimb as the high pressure system establishes itself and \nwidespread Major HeatRisk levels are expected by Friday and \nespecially Saturday. Widespread high temperatures of 100 to 105 \ndegrees are expected for Saturday, which is 10 to 15 degrees above\nnormal for this time of year. Low temperatures will be in the \n60s, somewhat limiting overnight recoveries. While the heat is \nexpected to be at its worst on Saturday, long term outlooks \nindicate it\u2019s not leaving any time soon, with intense heat favored\nthrough mid to late next week.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Low confidence in storm\npotential today and tomorrow. High confidence for smoke impacts\nthrough at least Thursday. High confidence in extreme heat,\nparticularly for this weekend. No changes were made to NBM\noutput.",
